@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
' purpose: where a secret RESTS between runs, and how it reaches a run without ever landing on disk
' session: d2ff9d04-cba3-46b5-87d6-698c921c9ede
' PARTLY APPLIED. _spec/_plans/ is exempt from the SPEC-reference rule.
'
' ── THE LEDGER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' DONE — shipped for macOS, and measured rather than inferred:
'   (a) at rest ....... ""internal/secretref"": ""keychain:"" · ""cmd:"" · ""env:"" ·
'                       ""pass:"", resolved host-side once per run, bounded,
'                       with the full failure taxonomy below
'   (c) own environ ... ""credentials.ChildEnv"" hands the value to the ONE
'                       launch exec on ""cmd.Env"". The os.Setenv hydration that
'                       put it in /proc/<pid>/environ is gone
'   the host store .... read, parsed, REPORTED and dropped. It decides nothing,
'                       which is what keeps it clear of the sbx inversion
'   the record ........ _spec/internal/secretref/secret-references.puml
'   the notes below marked APPLIED / CORRECTED / REFUTED say which of this
'   plan's original claims survived contact with a real Mac.
'
' REMAINS:
'   (d) mounted file .. BLOCKED — see "the prerequisite" below, which MOVED:
'                       retire-dind landed and kept the docker backend, so (d)
'                       now waits on deleting it. ""WriteBrokerEnv"" still writes
'                       ""broker.env"" on that path
'   the Linux half .... ""secret-service:"", systemd-creds and keyctl are
'                       unbuilt; ""keychain:"" answers Unsupported off darwin and
'                       names ""cmd:"" instead. The ranking in the legend is the
'                       design, not the state
'   the posture line .. nothing names a plaintext key once per run with the
'                       command that moves it into a store
'
' ── THE PREREQUISITE MOVED: retire-dind LANDED AND (d) IS STILL OPEN ───────
' (d) is not a coding gap, it is a BACKEND gap. On docker the agent's credential
' IS ~/.claude/.credentials.json in the mounted proveo home, so any path that
' carries a login there writes a plaintext token into a bind-mounted directory
' every later run can read — the one shape this plan refuses outright. On sbx
' that file is written INSIDE the sandbox by sbx's own proxy, holding
' proxy-managed sentinels while the real tokens stay in the egress layer.
'
' So the exposure closes by REMOVING the backend, not by patching it. retire-dind
' has now landed — the privileged sidecar is deleted and every def declares
' `docker: sbx` — but it deliberately KEPT docker+egress as the PROVEO_SBX=0
' fallback, which is where (d) lives. So the prerequisite is not "retire the
' sidecar" but "delete the backend", and that is still a decision this plan has no
' standing to make. What it can do is refuse to make the exposure worse.
'
'   what landed, and what it left _spec/_plans/retire-dind.puml (APPLIED)
'   why the blob cannot reach sbx _spec/internal/sbx/oauth-provisioning.puml
'   what shipped ................ _spec/internal/secretref/secret-references.puml
'   what the agent may hold ..... _spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary.puml
'   the sbx credential path ..... _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the egress tiers ............ _spec/internal/egress/egress-tiers.puml
'   the picker's credentials row  _spec/internal/choiceui/topology-states.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
title secret custody — (a) and (c) closed on macOS; (d) waits on deleting the docker backend

component "**(a) at rest between runs**\nplaintext in .env, or exported in a shell rc" as REST <<error>>
component "**(b) held by the agent**\nthe model's own process can read it" as HELD <<error>>
component "**(c) in argv**\nvisible in ps, and in --print output" as ARGV <<error>>
component "**(d) in a mounted file**\nany container with the mount reads it" as FILE <<error>>

node "host secret store — the gap this plan fills" as STORE <<host>> {
  component "darwin: Keychain\n""security find-generic-password""" as KC <<app>>
  component "linux: no single answer\nsee the ranking below" as LX <<app>>
  component "secretref\n""keychain:"" · ""pass:"" · ""cmd:"" · ""env:""" as REF <<app>>
}

component "proveo process\nresolves host-side; ChildEnv hands it to ONE exec" as PROC <<app>>
component "bare ""-e NAME""\nthe child inherits; argv carries the NAME only" as BARE <<app>>
component "sbx secret set\nvalue on stdin, never a flag" as SBXSEC <<app>>
component "the broker\nkey stays in the egress layer, injected at the MITM" as BROKER <<app>>
component "the agent" as AGENT <<app>>
cloud "the provider" as PROV <<cloud>>

REF ARROW_MAIN KC
REF ARROW_MAIN LX
KC ARROW_MAIN PROC : read at RUN TIME
LX ARROW_MAIN PROC
PROC ARROW_MAIN BARE
PROC ARROW_MAIN SBXSEC
PROC ARROW_MAIN BROKER
BROKER ARROW_CLOUD PROV : the agent never holds it
BARE ARROW_OPTIONAL AGENT : only under ""--credentials forward""
REF ARROW_ERROR REST : what a store replaces
BROKER ARROW_ERROR HELD : what brokering replaces
BARE ARROW_ERROR ARGV : what a bare name replaces
PROC ARROW_ERROR FILE : NOT closed today — see WriteBrokerEnv

note top of PROC
  **Read it to RUN the container, never to write
  a file.** Materialising a Keychain value into
  the proveo home would put a plaintext token in
  a bind-mounted directory — recreating the exact
  exposure the Keychain exists to prevent.
end note

note bottom of PROC
  **"In memory" WAS an overstatement.**
  ""HydrateProcessEnv"" called ""os.Setenv"", so
  the value entered proveo's own environ and
  every later child inherited it. Removed.
end note

note bottom of PROC
  **APPLIED — it is set on the CHILD.**
  ""credentials.ChildEnv"" collects the pairs;
  ""cmd.Env"" carries them to the one exec that
  forwards them, on both backends. ""--print""
  is unaffected: the argv never reads them.
end note

note bottom of BARE
  **The value never enters argv.** A bare
  ""-e NAME"" makes the child inherit it from
  proveo's own environment, so ""ps"" sees a name
  and no value. This already ships; a store
  feeds it rather than replacing it.
end note

note bottom of BROKER
  **A store and the broker solve DIFFERENT
  halves.** The store decides where the secret
  sleeps; the broker decides who is awake with
  it. Neither substitutes for the other, and
  broker stays the default.
end note

note top of REF
  **APPLIED (macOS). One reference, many
  stores.** ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=keychain:"" reads
  it where the operator already keeps it, so
  proveo adopts a store rather than becoming
  one — nothing to migrate, nothing to back up.
end note

note bottom of REF
  **APPLIED. ""cmd:"" is the escape hatch.**
  Linux has no single Keychain, so a resolver
  that runs the operator's own command covers
  1Password, Bitwarden and Vault without
  proveo integrating with any of them.
end note

note top of LX
  **STILL UNBUILT.** ""keychain:"" answers
  Unsupported off darwin and names the escape
  hatch; ""secret-service:"" is deliberately
  absent rather than guessed at.
end note

note top of LX
  **No Linux store is free the way Keychain
  is.** Each trades away headless use, or
  reboot survival, or ubiquity. The plan ranks
  them and lets the operator choose, rather
  than picking one and calling it the answer.
end note

note bottom of REST
  **REMAINS — a plaintext key is a posture, so
  say so.** proveo should name it once per run
  with the command that moves it — not refuse,
  because an operator mid-task with a working
  key is not helped by a tool that stops.
end note

note bottom of FILE
  **A secret in the proveo HOME is the worst
  shape.** Readable by every run that mounts it,
  outliving the run that made it — the failure a
  Keychain-backed host had already avoided, and
  the one this plan refuses outright.
end note

note bottom of FILE
  **But (d) is already open on the default path.**
  ""WriteBrokerEnv"" puts every provider key as
  plaintext in ""broker.env"" and mounts that into
  the proxy. Better than the home — 0600, per-run,
  outside every agent mount — but it is disk.
end note

note top of FILE
  **REMAINS — the mechanism exists, unused.**
  ""broker.Route"" takes a ""Value"" outranking
  ""ValueFile"", so the proxy can read the secret
  from its own environment. Closing this makes
  ""credentials"" a package with no writer at all.
end note

note top of FILE
  **STILL BLOCKED, one step further on.** On sbx
  the agent never reads a host credential file.
  retire-dind landed but kept docker as the
  PROVEO_SBX=0 fallback, so (d) now waits on
  deleting that backend, not on the sidecar.
end note

note top of KC
  **APPLIED — read, never written.** proveo
  reads what ""claude"" stored and never calls
  ""add-generic-password"": a tool that writes
  owns a secret's lifetime, and this one only
  borrows a value for one run.
end note

note bottom of KC
  **APPLIED — the algorithm is known.**
  ""Claude Code"" + suffix + ""-credentials"" +
  sha256(configDir NFC)[:8], account ""$USER""
  validated. Recomputed, never hardcoded, and
  taken only when the payload parses.
end note

note bottom of LX
  **Send the REFRESH token too — this is not a
  trade to make.** An access token alone gives the
  agent hours and no way to renew, so a long
  session dies mid-task on an auth error proveo
  caused. Carry the whole credential.
end note

note top of LX
  **CORRECTED — sbx has no writable slot.**
  The blob IS the right unit, and sbx already
  holds one: its ""anthropic"" service writes
  "".credentials.json"" inside the sandbox with
  proxy-managed sentinels.
end note

note top of LX
  **But proveo cannot fill it.** The
  ""--oauth"" flag is openai-only, and setting
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" would store JSON
  where a bearer token goes. Hence
  read-and-report on macOS.
end note

note bottom of KC
  **REFUTED — there is no dialog.** ""claude""
  stores the item BY SHELLING OUT to
  ""security add-generic-password -X"", so that
  binary is already the sole trusted app for
  decrypt and proveo's first read is silent.
end note

note bottom of KC
  **So proveo inherits the widening, never
  causes it.** What ships instead is an
  announcement before the exec — true on a
  host provisioned some other way, harmless
  on one that was not.
end note

legend bottom
  **The Linux ranking — headless · reboot · no daemon · ubiquity**
  ==
  **systemd-creds** — TPM2-sealed, host-bound, ships with systemd, no session needed.
  Closest to "free". Cost: sealing wants root, and the secret cannot leave the machine.
  --
  **pass (GPG)** — headless, survives reboot, scriptable, syncable. Cost: GPG setup is
  a real barrier, and pinentry over SSH needs configuring before it works once.
  --
  **Secret Service (libsecret)** — the true Keychain analogue, unlocks with the login
  session. Cost: needs a D-Bus session AND an unlocked keyring, so plain SSH and CI both fail.
  --
  **cmd: (1Password · Bitwarden · Vault · keepassxc-cli)** — whatever the operator
  already runs. Cost: a dependency proveo does not control, and a subprocess per resolve.
  --
  **A worked example — KeePassXC.** On LINUX it already speaks Secret Service (opt-in
  per database group), so ""secret-service:"" reads it with no proveo-specific work and
  KeePassXC raises its own per-application prompt. On macOS it does not, so the paths
  are ""cmd:keepassxc-cli show -a Password db.kdbx entry"" — which reopens the database
  every resolve rather than talking to the running instance — or copying the few keys
  proveo needs into the Keychain. The browser protocol (keepassxc-proxy, a libsodium
  handshake and an association key) is a third door and the wrong one: built for
  browsers, and it buys nothing the two above do not.
  --
  **kernel keyring (keyctl)** — in memory, never on disk. Not a store: it does not
  survive a reboot. Useful only as a cache in front of one of the above.
  --
  **age / sops** — good for secrets shared through a repo. Cost: the identity file is
  itself a plaintext key on disk unless it is sealed by one of the above.
  ==
  **env: and .env** — the status quo, and the thing this plan exists to make avoidable.
  Kept as a resolver so nothing breaks, warned once per run, never the default.
end legend
@enduml

' ── WHAT ONLY A macOS OPERATOR COULD SETTLE — SETTLED ─────────────────────
' Measured on darwin 26.6.2 / claude 2.1.259 / sbx 0.39.0. The four questions
' this block used to ask are answered in full, with the argv and the ACL, in
' _spec/internal/secretref/secret-references.puml. In brief:
'
'   1. svce="Claude Code-credentials", acct=$USER. `dump-keychain` prompts for
'      nothing. 2. The payload is raw JSON — the WHOLE credential, access and
'      refresh tokens with both stamps — and `-a` is not required.
'   3. NO dialog is raised, because the item's ACL already trusts
'      /usr/bin/security: `claude` created it through that binary.
'   4. Therefore the deny and no-GUI strings could not be provoked without
'      putting a modal on the operator's screen, and were not. The classifier
'      keys on the measured not-found line plus the documented interaction
'      wording, and QUOTES `security` verbatim for anything else.
'
' ── FAILURE TAXONOMY, AND THE RULE THAT GOVERNS IT ─────────────────────────
' A denial must never leave the run WORSE than not having asked. Every outcome
' below is a warning and a no-op: no file, no override, no suppression change.
'
'   not found   → try the next candidate, then name PROVEO_KEYCHAIN_SERVICE
'   denied      → "you denied access — continuing with the credential you had"
'   no GUI      → "this session cannot ask (ssh/CI) — export the token instead"
'   timed out   → a bounded exec is mandatory; an unanswered modal otherwise
'                 hangs proveo before launch with no output at all
'
' ── WHERE THE RESOLVER RUNS: SHIPPED ───────────────────────────────────────
' HOST-side, in the proveo process, once per run. Only the host has the
' Keychain, the D-Bus session, the unlocked KeePassXC and the operator to
' prompt; an agent inside the sandbox has none of them and must never be the
' thing that asks.
'
' ── WHERE THE VALUE GOES: HALF SHIPPED ─────────────────────────────────────
' The two units travel differently, and only one of them has an answer today.
'
'   an API key      SHIPPED, both backends. Resolved into the run's credential
'                   lookup, then handed to the ONE launch exec on cmd.Env.
'                   Never argv, never proveo's environ, never a file.
'   the login blob  REPORTED ONLY. sbx exposes no slot proveo can write
'                   (`secret set --oauth` is openai-only) and the docker home
'                   write is refused, so proveo names what the store holds and
'                   which backend could use it. Routing it is retire-dind's
'                   job, not this plan's.
'
' Neither unit puts a value in argv, and neither needs the agent to hold one.
' The agent only ever holds a credential under --credentials forward, which is a
' separate decision the operator makes on a separate row.
'
' ── THE INVERSION TO AVOID ON sbx — AVOIDED, AND PINNED ────────────────────
' On the sbx backend the login file is NOT read — sbx's own proxy materialises
' the credential inside its sandbox. So hydrating a file there is worse than
' doing nothing: PersistedLogin flips true, AuthSuppressor drops the anthropic
' env vars, Spec() then skips addSecret, and the sandbox gets NOTHING.
'
' The shipped resolver stays clear of it by deciding nothing: the host store
' feeds neither PersistedLogin nor AuthSuppressor, and
' TestKeychainNeverFeedsSuppression fails if it ever does. Whatever a LATER
' change hands back on sbx must go to `sbx secret set` (stdin), never to a
' file. This is still the sharpest hazard in the design.

' ── WHY THIS PLAN IS A PLAN AND NOT A PATCH ────────────────────────────────
' Written from inside a proveo sandbox: Linux, no `security`, no Keychain, no
' D-Bus, no KeePassXC, no host. Every claim about proveo's own source was read
' and verified here; every claim about a secret store was not, and is marked as
' inference where it matters. The agent in the sandbox is the wrong side of the
' boundary to test the boundary — which is itself the reason the resolver runs
' host-side rather than in here.

' ── THE REFRESH TOKEN IS PART OF THE CREDENTIAL ────────────────────────────
' Passing only the access token is the newbie mistake this plan must not make.
' It works on the bench — the agent authenticates, the run starts — and then
' the session outlives the token and dies on a 401 in the middle of somebody's
' task, with proveo as the sole cause. An hour-long ceiling on a coding session
' is not a security property, it is an outage with a countdown.
'
' So the unit that crosses the boundary is the whole credential — access token,
' refresh token, and both expiry stamps — never a bare string:
'
'   sbx      `sbx secret set --force NAME` with the BLOB on stdin; sbx's own
'            proxy materialises it inside the sandbox, where a file is behind
'            the boundary rather than on the host
'   docker   the agent reads ~/.claude/.credentials.json from the mounted home,
'            so carrying a refresh token there means a host file unless that
'            path is a tmpfs written from inside the container at start. THIS
'            IS THE OPEN PROBLEM — and on macOS it is why the resolver REPORTS
'            rather than routes. It remains the strongest argument for the
'            sbx-only direction in _spec/_plans/retire-dind.puml
'
' A corollary worth stating: whatever the agent renews INTO must persist, or
' the next run starts from the same stale credential and refreshes again. On
' sbx that is sbx's store; on docker it is the mounted home. Renewal that is
' thrown away at teardown is renewal the operator pays for twice.
